+# Team-scoped user management
+
+Use this reference when a Mattermost bot assigns application roles or access to existing Mattermost users.
+
+## Identity and authorization boundaries
+
+- Treat `User.id` as the stable identity. Show `@username` and an optional real name, but never accept a separately typed ID/username pair.
+- Store application roles such as `admin`, `operator`, or `viewer` in the application's repository. Do not mutate Mattermost passwords, account status, system roles, team roles, or membership unless the user explicitly requested native Mattermost administration.
+- Revalidate the selected ID against the configured team when the form is submitted. A signed or native select prevents casual mistakes but is not an authorization boundary.
+- Exclude bot accounts, deactivated users, and users already assigned to the application unless the task explicitly needs them.
+- Preserve a last-active-application-admin invariant when the application has its own administrators.
+
+## Reading one team's users
+
+Use the native paged endpoint with the runtime bot token:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v4/users?in_team=<team_id>&active=true&page=0&per_page=100
+```
+
+Page until the result is shorter than `per_page`. Reject malformed profiles or duplicate IDs instead of silently producing an ambiguous picker.
+
+Mattermost form `data_source="users"` searches users visible to the client and is not a strict one-team filter. For an exact team boundary, build static options from the endpoint above or implement a dynamic lookup that applies the same `in_team` filter and submit-time validation.
+
+For a small team, rebuild static options whenever the form opens; no cache or lookup endpoint is necessary. For a large team, prefer server-side search/dynamic lookup and bounded results.
+
+## Giving the bot access
+
+Provision membership separately from runtime:
+
+1. A setup credential with `add_user_to_team` adds the bot account through `POST /api/v4/teams/{team_id}/members`.
+2. Verify the resulting bot/team membership by calling the team-scoped users endpoint with the ordinary bot token.
+3. Run the application with the ordinary bot token. Do not grant system-admin or add an administrative runtime fallback merely to populate a user picker.
+
+The endpoint requires an active session and membership in the selected team; it does not require system-admin. Customized roles or access policies can still affect the bot, so treat the real startup request as authoritative and fail explicitly when it is denied.
+
+A DM/direct channel has no trustworthy team scope. Supply the configured team ID through deployment/provisioning or let an authorized administrator choose from an allowlist; never trust an arbitrary team ID from form submission.
+
+## Recommended flow
+
+For a small application-admin panel:
+
+1. Open Users.
+2. Open Add user.
+3. Choose an unassigned active human team member, choose an application role, and submit.
+
+This keeps the primary task at three clicks from persistent navigation, keeps the native form to three fields or fewer, and avoids exposing raw IDs or password controls.
